<BaseLayout title="Hartree and Hartree–Fock Theory" description="Product states, Slater determinants, mean fields, exchange, orbital equations, and their limits." current="theory">
  <nav class="breadcrumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
    <a href={href('/theory/')}>Theory</a><span aria-hidden="true">/</span><span aria-current="page">Hartree and Hartree–Fock Theory</span>
  </nav>

  <h1>Hartree and Hartree–Fock Theory</h1>
  <p class="lede">Hartree and Hartree–Fock replace the unrestricted many-electron wavefunction by orbital-based trial states. The simplification is decisive: an exponentially large variational problem becomes a nonlinear set of one-electron equations.</p>
  <p>The price is equally precise. Hartree neglects fermionic antisymmetry at the wavefunction level; Hartree–Fock enforces antisymmetry with one determinant but cannot describe correlation that requires a superposition of determinants.</p>

  <h2>Hartree begins with a product state</h2>
  <p>The Hartree ansatz assigns each electron a one-electron orbital and forms a simple product:</p>
  <div class="math-display">
    <math display="block" aria-label="Psi Hartree equals product over electrons of phi sub i of x sub i">
      <semantics>
        <mrow>
          <msub><mi>Ψ</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo>…</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>)</mo>
          <mo>=</mo><munderover><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>)</mo><mo>.</mo>
        </mrow>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi_&#123;\mathrm&#123;H&#125;&#125;(x_1,\ldots,x_N)=\prod_&#123;i=1&#125;^&#123;N&#125;\phi_i(x_i).</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>Each orbital moves in the external potential and in an average Coulomb field generated by the other orbitals. The equations are coupled because changing any orbital changes that field.</p>
  <p>A raw product does not change sign when two electron labels are exchanged, so it is not a valid general fermionic state. Hartree theory is therefore mainly valuable as the transparent starting point for mean-field reasoning.</p>

  <h2>A determinant enforces antisymmetry</h2>
  <p>Hartree–Fock restricts the trial state to one normalized Slater determinant of spin orbitals:</p>
  <div class="math-display">
    <math display="block" aria-label="Phi equals one over square root N factorial times determinant of spin orbitals">
      <semantics>
        <mrow>
          <mi>Φ</mi><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo>…</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>)</mo>
          <mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msqrt><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>!</mo></mrow></msqrt></mfrac>
          <mo>det</mo><mo>[</mo><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo>)</mo><mo>]</mo><mo>.</mo>
        </mrow>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi(x_1,\ldots,x_N)=\frac&#123;1&#125;&#123;\sqrt&#123;N!&#125;&#125;\det[\phi_p(x_q)].</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>Antisymmetry is then exact within the ansatz. Exchange terms appear automatically when the expectation value of the electron–electron interaction is evaluated. No empirical “exchange force” is added.</p>

  <h2>The energy separates Coulomb and exchange contributions</h2>
  <p>For orthonormal occupied spin orbitals, the single-determinant energy has the form</p>
  <div class="math-display">
    <math display="block" aria-label="Hartree Fock energy equals sum of one electron terms plus one half sum of Coulomb minus exchange integrals">
      <semantics>
        <mrow>
          <msub><mi>E</mi><mi>HF</mi></msub><mo>=</mo>
          <munderover><mo>∑</mo><mi>i</mi><mi>N</mi></munderover><mo>⟨</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>|</mo><mover><mi>h</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>|</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>⟩</mo>
          <mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac>
          <munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover>
          <mo>(</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>)</mo><mo>.</mo>
        </mrow>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_&#123;\mathrm&#123;HF&#125;&#125;=\sum_i^N\langle i|\hat&#123;h&#125;|i\rangle+\frac&#123;1&#125;&#123;2&#125;\sum_&#123;ij&#125;^N(J_&#123;ij&#125;-K_&#123;ij&#125;).</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p><math class="math-inline" aria-label="J sub i j"><semantics><msub><mi>J</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_&#123;ij&#125;</annotation></semantics></math> is the classical-looking Coulomb integral between orbital densities. <math class="math-inline" aria-label="K sub i j"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_&#123;ij&#125;</annotation></semantics></math> is the exchange integral generated by antisymmetry. Exchange acts only between identical spin components in the usual spin-orbital expression.</p>
  <p>The self-Coulomb contribution of an occupied spin orbital is exactly cancelled by its self-exchange term. This cancellation is an exact property of the single-determinant expression, not a general statement that Hartree–Fock has no approximation error.</p>

  <h2>Variational minimization produces orbital equations</h2>
  <p>Minimizing the determinant energy while preserving orbital orthonormality yields the Hartree–Fock equations. In canonical form,</p>
  <div class="math-display">
    <math display="block" aria-label="Fock operator acting on phi i equals epsilon i phi i">
      <semantics>
        <mrow><mover><mi>f</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo></mrow>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat&#123;f&#125;\phi_i=\varepsilon_i\phi_i,</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>with</p>
  <div class="math-display">
    <math display="block" aria-label="Fock operator equals one electron operator plus sum of Coulomb minus exchange operators">
      <semantics>
        <mrow>
          <mover><mi>f</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mover><mi>h</mi><mo>^</mo></mover>
          <mo>+</mo><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>occ</mi></munderover>
          <mo>(</mo><mover><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><mover><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>)</mo><mo>.</mo>
        </mrow>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat&#123;f&#125;=\hat&#123;h&#125;+\sum_&#123;j\in\mathrm&#123;occ&#125;&#125;(\hat&#123;J&#125;_j-\hat&#123;K&#125;_j).</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>The Fock operator depends on the occupied orbitals that it is used to determine. Hartree–Fock is consequently a nonlinear self-consistent problem, even though each fixed Fock operator defines a linear eigenvalue equation.</p>

  <h2>The occupied subspace is more fundamental than canonical orbitals</h2>
  <p>A unitary rotation among fully occupied orbitals changes their individual shapes but leaves the determinant, one-particle density matrix, and Hartree–Fock energy unchanged up to an overall phase. Canonical orbitals are the representation that diagonalizes the Fock matrix within the relevant spaces; they are not the only legitimate orbital representation.</p>
  <p>This invariance underlies localized occupied orbitals and explains why an individual occupied orbital shape should not automatically be treated as a unique observable.</p>

  <h2>Closed-shell and open-shell choices are different ansätze</h2>
  <dl class="paired-definitions">
    <dt>Restricted Hartree–Fock</dt>
    <dd>Paired electrons share spatial orbitals. This preserves a simple spin structure but can become qualitatively poor when bonds stretch or near-degeneracy develops.</dd>
    <dt>Unrestricted Hartree–Fock</dt>
    <dd>Different spin channels use different spatial orbitals. This allows spin polarization and symmetry breaking but may produce a state that is not an eigenfunction of total spin.</dd>
    <dt>Restricted open-shell Hartree–Fock</dt>
    <dd>Open-shell occupations are treated while retaining specified spin adaptation. The orbital equations and canonicalization conventions are less unique than in the closed-shell case.</dd>
  </dl>
  <p>Lowering the variational energy by breaking spatial or spin symmetry can reveal a limitation of the restricted ansatz. It does not by itself establish that the broken-symmetry determinant is the exact physical state.</p>

  <h2>Hartree–Fock includes exchange but misses correlation beyond one determinant</h2>
  <p>Within a chosen one-electron basis, Hartree–Fock is the best variational single determinant. The remaining difference from the exact nonrelativistic ground-state energy is commonly called the correlation energy relative to the Hartree–Fock reference.</p>
  <p>This definition is method- and basis-aware. Basis incompleteness, relativistic effects, nuclear motion, and model choices are separate error sources. Static correlation can also make the single-determinant reference qualitatively wrong, so a small perturbative correction is not guaranteed to repair it.</p>

  <h2>Orbital energies have limited interpretation</h2>
  <p>Koopmans' theorem relates a canonical occupied-orbital energy to a frozen-orbital ionization estimate. The approximation neglects orbital relaxation and correlation. Empty-orbital energies have still weaker general addition-energy meaning.</p>
  <aside class="callout">
    <p>A Hartree–Fock eigenvalue is not generally an exact electron-addition or electron-removal energy.</p>
    <p>A successful self-consistent iteration does not establish basis convergence, stability of the determinant, or accuracy for a target observable.</p>
  </aside>

  <h2>Where this page connects</h2>
  <p><a href={href('/theory/many-electron-problem/')}>The Many-Electron Problem</a> defines the unrestricted interacting system. <a href={href('/theory/density-functional-theory-foundations/')}>Density Functional Theory: Foundations</a> changes the basic variational variable from a determinant to the density. <a href={href('/theory/exchange-correlation-functionals-and-approximations/')}>Exchange–Correlation Functionals and Approximations</a> distinguishes exact Hartree–Fock exchange from practical Kohn–Sham and generalized Kohn–Sham approximation families, while <a href={href('/theory/self-consistent-field-methods/')}>Self-Consistent Field Methods</a> separates nonlinear orbital-equation convergence from representation convergence and scientific support.</p>

  <h2>Study routes</h2>
  <p><a href="https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/5-61-physical-chemistry-fall-2017/">MIT OpenCourseWare 5.61, Physical Chemistry</a> is the free bridge from quantum mechanics and molecular orbitals to many-electron atoms, basis sets, and electronic correlation. It provides lecture notes, videos, problem sets, solutions, and exams, but it is not a complete derivation of modern Hartree–Fock implementations.</p>
  <p><a href="https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/book/10.1002/9781119019572">Helgaker, Jørgensen, and Olsen, <cite>Molecular Electronic-Structure Theory</cite></a> is the rigorous reference for second quantization, molecular integrals, Hartree–Fock theory, correlated methods, and calibration. It is commercial or institutionally accessed.</p>
  <p><a href="https://www.cambridge.org/core/books/abs/electronic-structure/theoretical-background/77B94007F143F1D85E7D96B13237F62A">Richard M. Martin, <cite>Electronic Structure</cite>, 2nd ed., Chapter 3</a> places Hartree–Fock beside independent-electron, density-based, and correlated descriptions for both finite and extended systems.</p>
  <p>The open-source Psi4 project’s <a href="https://psicode.org/psi4manual/1.8.x/index_tutorials.html">Hartree–Fock tutorial</a> is a small executable bridge from equations to a molecular SCF calculation. It shows closed-shell RHF and open-shell UHF input, charge and multiplicity, a declared Gaussian basis, and the distinction between an energy calculation and a geometry optimization. Use it after learning determinants and exchange to make the variables concrete; its defaults, thresholds, and molecular examples are code- and problem-specific, not a validation recipe for a calculation.</p>

  <aside class="review-note">
    <p><strong>Review note.</strong> Scope and resource roles were reviewed on 2 August 2026. The page distinguishes exact antisymmetry, single-determinant variational optimality, basis error, self-consistent convergence, and correlation beyond the Hartree–Fock reference.</p>
  </aside>
</BaseLayout>
